2026-05-09  Commit B: dual-scale zoom on placeholder grid (Nail #1)
  WHAT: Five new files in swarm/src/
        - camera.ts      Pure math. zoom + panX + panY state.
                         zoomAt(factor, screenX, screenY) keeps the
                         world point under the cursor/finger fixed
                         across the zoom (focal-point zoom). panBy
                         translates in world space.
        - placeholder.ts 50x50 grid in 3 hashed colors (grass/sand/
                         water). Strategic layer = flat fills.
                         Detail layer = grid lines (1px, alpha 0.45)
                         + center dots (r=2, alpha 0.6).
        - input.ts       Pointer events: 1-finger drag = pan,
                         2-finger pinch = zoom around midpoint.
                         Wheel = zoom around cursor (1.0015^-deltaY,
                         smooth on trackpad). Keyboard +/- = zoom
                         around screen center.
        - store.ts       Zustand vanilla store. Holds the readout
                         state (zoom, view label, crossfade). Used
                         by the DOM readout subscriber, set by the
                         tick when values change meaningfully
                         (deadband to avoid 60Hz updates).
        - main.ts        Boots Pixi, builds the world container,
                         wires camera + placeholder + input. Per
                         tick: applies camera transform, computes
                         crossfade from cellPixelSize (24px = 0,
                         64px = 1), sets detailLayer.alpha, pushes
                         throttled state to readoutStore.
  WHY:  Nail #1 done end-to-end per port.md "Nail-1 spec":
        - Placeholder content: 50x50 hashed grid, 3 colors. ✓
        - Smooth continuous zoom + crossfade between strategic
          (flat colors) and tactical (lines + dots overlay). ✓
        - Pinch + wheel + keyboard +/-. ✓
        - Single-finger drag = pan. ✓
        Bundle ~210 KB gz. Per the mantra: this is one aspect,
        end-to-end, before anything else. Next aspect (map gen,
        soldiers, selection, etc.) waits for user thumbs-up here.

